# Quanteda tutorials

<p class="subtitle">A hands-on introduction to quantitative text analysis with R</p>


{{% author %}}
[Kohei Watanabe](https://koheiw.net) and [Stefan Müller](https://stefanmueller.ie)
{{% /author %}}


The site covers the fundamentals of text analysis with **quanteda**: R basics, importing text data, and the package's core objects, the corpus, tokens, and document-feature matrix, followed by advanced operations, such as document classification, text scaling, unsupervised clustering, word embeddings, and how to connect **quanteda** with tools for large language model classification. The site is not a textbook on the statistics or theory behind these methods. The aim is to get you running real analyses and curious to learn more.

**quanteda** and associated packages have been actively maintained since in 2012. Quantitative text analysis, machine learning, and natural language processing have all evolved rapidly since. The **quanteda** package infrastructure works alongside embedding- and LLM-based tools.

The site has over 30 sections. Click a chapter name on the left to expand its sections, or use the "Search" field in the top-left corner to search for topics or R functions.

The **quanteda** team created this site for workshops and for users who want a step-by-step introduction to text analysis using R. We have also created several additional useful [resources](https://quanteda.io), such as vignettes, replications, a cheatsheet and a comparison to functions in **quanteda** and other packages for quantitative text analysis.

Examples in this tutorial are written for **quanteda** version `r packageVersion("quanteda")`. Please check if you have the same version installed by a command `packageVersion("quanteda")`.

You can run every R command yourself: [download the source code of this website](https://github.com/quanteda/tutorials.quanteda.io/archive/master.zip) from the [Github repository](https://github.com/quanteda/tutorials.quanteda.io), unzip it, and open `quanteda.tutorials.Rproj` in your R IDE of choice. The executable R commands are in the `.Rmarkdown` files under the `content` folder.

{{% notice note %}}
If you use **quanteda** or these tutorials in your own work, we would appreciate a citation:
- Watanabe, Kohei, and Stefan Müller. 2026. *Quanteda Tutorials*. <https://tutorials.quanteda.io>.
- Benoit, Kenneth, Kohei Watanabe, Haiyan Wang, Paul Nulty, Adam Obeng, Stefan Müller, and Akitaka Matsuo. 2018. "quanteda: An R Package for the Quantitative Analysis of Textual Data." *Journal of Open Source Software* 3 (30): 774. <https://doi.org/10.21105/joss.00774>.
{{% /notice %}}

## Contributions and Questions

Contributions in the form of feedback, comments, code, and bug reports are most welcome. If you want to change the content of the pages, please edit the `.Rmarkdown` (instead of `.markdown`) files in the `content` folder and issue a pull request on [Github](https://github.com/quanteda/tutorials.quanteda.io).

If you have questions on how to use **quanteda**, please post them to [the quanteda channel on StackOverflow](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/tagged/quanteda). If you find a bug, please report it to the [quanteda issues](https://github.com/quanteda/quanteda/issues). *We prefer these platforms to emails in communicating with our users* because the records will help other users who face similar problems.

